  "conclusions": [
    "The source-frozen B.1 thin-band luminosity and the Rmax=25 km high-energy B.2 luminosity reproduce the archived P1-C baseline at floating-point precision.",
    "For the selected 210--510 MeV band, less than 1e-6 of the B.1 source lies outside 15 km; the inward boundary response is therefore an opacity-support effect, not source truncation.",
    "Moving Rmax inward from 25 to 24 km increases the high-energy escaped luminosity by 28.84%; moving it to 22 km increases it by 116.06%.",
    "Extending the trapping boundary from 25 to 26 km with the conditional vacuum-limit neutrino opacity decreases the band luminosity by 22.42%; extensions to 27.5 and 30 km decrease it by 46.64% and 70.94%.",
    "The 64-to-128 path-order change is below 8.7e-6 at all tested boundaries.",
    "The literal Rmax=25 km paper prescription remains the baseline; no boundary is selected by fitting Fig. 7."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Only the 210--510 MeV band at the 10 MeV strong-endpoint coupling is calculated; this is not a full 25-point Fig. 7 rerun.",
    "The B.1 source is frozen at its 25 km baseline. Its fraction outside inward Rmax=15 km is below 1e-6 in this band.",
    "The outward continuation is conditional on neutrino daughters remaining trapped beyond 25 km and uses the vacuum-limit two-flavour inverse-decay opacity with zero Compton opacity; it is not a reconstructed thermodynamic profile.",
    "The profile is a public-figure reconstruction, not the original Garching snapshot."
  ],
  "outward_extension_consistency_note": "Lai et al. (2024) require production and attenuation regions to be treated consistently. The Rmax>25 km rows are one-sided opacity-only diagnostics with the B.1 source frozen at 25 km, not self-consistent alternative luminosity predictions."
}
